[Music]  making the modern multiverse review of  the number of the heavens a history of  the multiverse and the quest to  understand the cosmos by Tom Siegfried  Harvard University Press 2019 one of the  liveliest scientific debates is whether  our universe is unique  unlike some controversies the multiverse  debate is highly interdisciplinary  philosophers theologians and of course  businesses all cling to entrenched  positions about the multiverse is  reality perhaps no other topic than the  multiverse has so clearly and  passionately raised the question of what  constitutes science into this debate  comes the number of the heavens a  history of the multiverse and the quest  to understand the cosmos by Tom  Siegfried the former editor-in-chief of  science news an author of three books  including strange matters undiscovered  ideas at the frontiers of space and time  2002 despite his journalistic  credentials Siegfried is not a  dispassionate observer or turgid he  boldly asserts his opinions as he  enumerates the many ways humanity has  understood the multiverse from antiquity  to today combining interviews of modern  physicists and philosophers with  detailed historical narrative of ancient  Medieval and Renaissance interpretations  of the word world Siegfried's text  fields an important gap in the expanding  body of multiverse literature his  approach to the multiverse is liberal  possible universe have at times included  other planets in our solar system other  stars in our galaxy planets around other  stars in our galaxy other galaxies in  our universe and parallel universes in  both space and time  Siegfried thus gives the interpretation  of multiverse a wide berth however only  the parallel universes interpretation is  relevant to modern physicists the number  of the heavens is intended for a general  audience the book's first 2/3 is replete  with ancient conceptions of the  multiverse though clearly the ancient  thinkers were considering other possibly  habitable planets rather than multiple  universes nowadays no scientist or  philosopher doubts the existence of such  world's even earth-like ones  whereas the evidence from multiple  universes is it as yet non-existent  Siegfried sees the modern interpretation  of the multiverse as an inevitable  logical evolution of Copernican thought  why would our universe be singular 7  other planets aside from earth populate  our solar system alone a hundred or  billion or more Suns in the Milky Way  and a similar number of galaxies exist  in the observable universe Siegfried  writes about many historical scientists  Copernicus included who entertained the  notion that some version of the  multiverse may exist the historical  prologue though interesting takes up  most of the book that leaves only a few  chapters for Sigrid's juiciest prose on  the modern meaning of the multiverse  Everett many worlds theory and the  inflationary multiverse the imbalance  between ancient and modern multiverse  conceptions isn't the books only a short  company most notably the perspective of  multiverse opponents are only minimally  covered as an experimental cosmologists  working to constrain models of inflation  I was disappointed not to find a  description of the most promising  approach to discovering evidence for  inflation and potentially the no divers  as well B mode polarization of the  Cosmic Microwave Background siegfried  reject science philosopher Karl poppers  idea that the demarcation between  scientific and unscientific theories  lies in the ability to be proven false  but Siegfried provides know all their  alternative rather he cites the opinions  of Stephen Weinberg Leonard Susskind  John Carroll Lisa Randall another mode  of multiverse proponents or he asserts  his own opinions  his referring to opponents of the  multiverse with the pejorative deniers  lengths them with deniers of evolution  climate change or worse a less partisan  presentation would have improved the  books balance funding multiverse  opponents is not exactly difficult and  informative if heated debate recently  took place between multiverse proponents  and opponents in reaction to the article  by M aegis Paul Steinhardt and Avraham  Loeb in the February 2017 issue of  Scientific American healthy debate is a  welcome feature of the  first landscape other books on the  multiverse include Brian Greene's the  hidden reality parallel universes of the  deep laws of the cosmos and Max tegmark  s-- our mathematical universe my quest  for the ultimate nature of reality those  texts largely eschew the history of the  multiverse can cover the pertinent if  esoteric physics more thoroughly the  number of the heavens shines when  Siegfried adopts a journalistic  neutrality about the arguments for and  against the multiverse his wry wit is  evident throughout but nowhere more so  than when relating past episodes of  confusing and even contradictory  interpretation of metaphysical ideas at  the outset siegfried stresses that there  is no greater story in science than the  human quest to comprehend the cosmos our  understanding of the universe is rapidly  expanding but physicists still debate  the definition of the words we use to  describe the cosmos as capacious  nosotras constantly evolves at least  with the number of the heavens we  finally know begin  [Music]  [Music]

